WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.000
When a maths problem gives you three numbers, each

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.550
number can do a different job.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:05.970
One might count packets.

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:09.040
Another might tell you how many counters fit in each

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:09.470
packet.

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:12.010
The third might count counters on their own.

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:14.870
Give every number its job before you calculate.

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:18.770
Start with the question: how many counters altogether?

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:21.350
That tells us the unit of our answer.

00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:25.210
Four plus three plus two gives nine, but we would be

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:27.990
adding a packet count to two different descriptions of

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:28.250
counters.

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:31.650
Give each quantity its label and ask what it would

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:33.540
take to get everything into counters.

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:36.330
Mia has four packets of counters.

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:40.100
Each packet contains three counters, and Mia has two

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:41.350
loose counters as well.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:43.830
How many counters does she have altogether?

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:46.920
Before you work it out, point to what the four, the

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:48.590
three and the two each describe.

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:53.410
Four tells us how many groups there are.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:55.750
Three tells us the size of every group.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:58.830
Open the first packet and you can see three counters.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:01.390
Open the others and the same group repeats.

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:04.250
Four groups of three give twelve counters.

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:06.940
Multiplication gives the counter total for the

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:07.270
packets.

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:10.850
The two loose counters are already in the unit we

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:11.240
want.

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:12.780
Slide them into the collection.

00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:16.000
Now we are adding twelve counters and two counters,

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:18.180
and the total is fourteen counters.

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:21.870
Check it in the picture: four rows of three, and two

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:22.580
beside them.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:25.070
The same idea works with measurements.

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:28.540
One ribbon is two metres long, and another is thirty

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:29.630
centimetres long.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:31.430
How much ribbon is there altogether?

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.580
Two metres is two hundred centimetres.

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:37.500
Both lengths are now in centimetres, so together they

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:40.130
measure two hundred and thirty centimetres.

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:43.050
A number can have a different job even when it uses

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:43.920
the same digits.

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:46.470
Think of a book with pages numbered ninety-five

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:47.600
through one hundred and five.

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:51.710
In just that range, how many times is the digit nine

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:52.180
written?

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:55.210
Look across the page numbers and find each printed

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:55.760
nine.

00:01:58.310 --> 00:02:01.560
Five page numbers contain a nine: ninety-five,

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:05.230
ninety-six, ninety-seven, ninety-eight and

00:02:05.230 --> 00:02:05.660
ninety-nine.

00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:09.190
The last page number on that list contains two nines.

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:12.730
Its first nine counts once and its second nine counts

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:13.180
once.

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:15.400
There are six written nines in all.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:17.630
Read the final question.

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:20.390
Label each number with what it counts or measures.

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:23.400
Bring quantities into a common unit when you need to

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:23.890
add them.

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:25.430
Here is your notebook challenge.

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:28.050
Six boxes hold eight pencils each.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:29.810
Five pencils are broken.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:32.230
How many unbroken pencils are there?

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:34.710
Label the quantities and show your calculation.

